    question: "Why does the same prompt give different answers each time?",
    answer: "Because models choose words by weighted dice roll, not lookup. At each step the model produces a probability for every possible next token, and the app samples from those probabilities — a setting called temperature controls how adventurous the sampling is. Ask twice, get two valid paths through the probability space. Even at temperature zero (always pick the most likely token), tiny numerical differences in how GPUs batch and order calculations can nudge a borderline choice, so perfect determinism isn't guaranteed either. What this is not: the model having moods, learning between your attempts, or being 'worn out.' Practical uses: for creative work, variation is the feature — regenerate freely. For factual questions, asking the same thing twice and comparing answers is a cheap hallucination check: confident consistency isn't proof, but confident inconsistency is a red flag.",
